  (:adr/body "# ADR 2799008183: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 8183 Packing, Bottling and Labelling Machine Operators plant scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 8183\n-- Packing, Bottling and Labelling Machine Operators** only. This is\nWave3 (production/trades); Wave0/1/2 are 100% complete, and Wave3\nbatches 1-14 had already landed 84 occupations before this batch, with\n17 gaps remaining (`kotoba.occupation.wave/wave-of \"8183\"` resolves to\nwave 3).\n\n`gh api repos/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8183` returned a clean\n404 before any work began -- this was a genuinely fresh scaffold, not a\nresume of prior partial work.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"8183\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"8183\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Packing, Bottling and Labelling\nMachine Operators\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to\nthis ADR the entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with\n`:required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no\n`:repo`/`:business-id`. This ADR and its companion registry edit\npromote the `8183` entry to `:implemented`.\n\n**Industrial packaging-line machine hazard dimension.** Packing,\nBottling and Labelling Machine Operators run high-speed packaging/\nbottling lines (case packers, bottle fillers/cappers, labelling\nmachines, wrapping and palletizing lines) -- a real heavy-machinery\nhazard domain: entanglement and crush hazard from conveyors and\npacking mechanisms. This actor does NOT model direct line-equipment\noperation authority or plant-safety-clearance authority. It is\ndeliberately restricted to PLANT SCHEDULING and LOGISTICS COORDINATION\naround the occupation (production-run/inventory/progress-record\nlogging, crew/shift scheduling, safety-concern escalation, and\npackaging-materials/consumables procurement coordination) -- never the\nline-operation-execution decision or the plant-safety-clearance\ndecision itself, and never an override of a plant safety officer's\njudgment. The closed proposal-op allowlist structurally excludes any\nop that could directly finalize such a decision, and the governor\nindependently, permanently hard-blocks any proposal whose rationale\ntext names such a finalization action, regardless of the declared\n`:op` or confidence -- this is not an escalation path a human can\napprove through, it is an outright, structural refusal at every\nmaturity level.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE, per this\nbatch's explicit task instruction, is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8143`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8143)\n(Paper Products Machine Operators -- a closely related industrial\nconverting-line safety pattern), independently re-cloned and read in\nfull this session. Its `papercoord.{store,advisor,governor,actor}`\nmodule split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph shape, closed op-allowlist\nand scope-exclusion self-trip-guard design translate almost 1:1 onto\nthis occupation's own heavy-machinery-hazard-adjacent coordination\nscope, so this ADR's `packcoord.*` modules follow the same shape with\ndomain terms substituted (converter -> packer, paper-converting\nfacility/plant -> packaging/bottling line facility, machine-operation-\nexecution -> line-operation-execution, converting/cutting/folding-run\n-> packing/bottling/labelling-run).\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8183` as an OSS\ndocumentation/logistics-coordination actor for packing, bottling and\nlabelling machine operators, following the SAME governed-actor\narchitecture as every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this\nfleet: **Packing, Bottling and Labelling Plant Scheduling Coordination\nAdvisor ⊣ PackCoordGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the\n`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family),\nwith an independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **plant scheduling/logistics coordination\nONLY, never packaging-line-equipment operation authority** -- it must\nNEVER be designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose\nexercising a line-operation-execution decision or a\nplant-safety-clearance decision.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- production-run/inventory/progress data\n  logging only\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/shift/task scheduling proposal\n  only\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a conveyor-entanglement/\n  machinery-condition/equipment-condition concern, ALWAYS escalates\n  immediately, NEVER auto-commit-eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- packaging-materials/consumables\n  procurement proposal only\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase,\nresembles a line-operation-execution finalization decision or a\nplant-safety-clearance finalization decision -- these are structurally\nabsent from the op-allowlist entirely, not merely gated behind\nescalation.\n\n### Five HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Packer unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered packer (crew member) record\n   (`:no-packer`). Never trusts the proposal's own claim of who the\n   packer is.\n2. **Facility unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered packaging/bottling line facility record\n   (`:no-facility`).\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (`:no-actuation`; the governor never dispatches hardware\n   itself and never operates packaging line equipment itself).\n4. **Op not allowed** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op\n   allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`).\n5. **Scope-excluded action** -- the proposal's `:op` or `:rationale`\n   must never name a line-operation-execution finalization, a\n   plant-safety-clearance finalization, or a plant-safety-officer-\n   judgment override (`:scope-excluded-action`), REGARDLESS of the\n   declared `:op` or confidence. This is a hard, PERMANENT block --\n   there is no maturity level or confidence at which this actor may\n   auto-commit or human-approve its way past this rule; it is an\n   outright refusal, not an escalation.\n\n### Escalation gates (always human sign-off)\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's always-escalate path). The only channel by\n  which the robot may surface a conveyor-entanglement/crush-hazard/\n  machinery-condition/equipment-condition observation -- it never\n  resolves the concern itself.\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000) -- always needs human sign-off,\n  independent of confidence (boundary verified as exclusive by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`: exactly-at-threshold\n  commits, over-threshold escalates).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, CLAUDE.md)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as bare NOUNS (e.g.\n\"conveyor\", \"bottle\", \"label\") can accidentally match inside the mock\nadvisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- and this vertical's domain\nvocabulary is an especially sharp case of that risk, because routine\nwork-log/task text legitimately names packaging-line equipment by name\n(e.g. a task literally described as \"routine packing task with\nconveyors, bottles and labels\" -- equipment identification, not a\nproposal to finalize a line operation or clear the plant as safe).\n`packcoord.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased exclusively as\nfinalization ACTION PHRASES (\"proceed with the packing run\",\n\"finalize the line operation decision\", \"declare the plant safety\ncleared\", \"override the plant safety officer's judgment\"), never bare\nnouns, and is checked only against the proposal's `:rationale` field\n(the advisor's own generated text), never against raw `:task` input\ntext. `packcoord.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-\nself-trip-on-scope-exclusion` is a dedicated regression test driving\nthe default mock-advisor across all four allowed ops with a task field\ncontaining exactly those bare equipment nouns, asserting none of them\never hard-trip the finalization-scope rule. Green on first run.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"8183\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8183` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-8183`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary` immediately before the edit,\n  not assumed, to account for concurrent sibling landings in this same\n  batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 30 tests, 65 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's `:test/\n  counts` and independently re-verified against a brand-new fresh\n  clone of the pushed repository (not just the local working copy). No\n  mojibake in the Japanese violation-detail strings (0\n  replacement-byte instances grepped in the fresh clone).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely\n  via the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent\n  with sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`) -- a single-entity EDN vector with an\n  `:adr/body` string blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-line-operation`/`:approve-\n  plant-safety-clearance` that is merely gated behind an\n  always-escalate path: rejected outright per the task's explicit\n  guidance -- an always-escalate op still means the *capability* to\n  finalize a line operation or a plant-safety clearance exists in the\n  code path and is merely deferred to a human gate. For a decision\n  whose failure mode is entanglement/crush injury from a high-speed\n  packaging/bottling line, the capability itself must not exist in\n  this actor at all.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"conveyor\" \"bottle\"\n  \"label\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class described above -- this domain's own vocabulary necessarily\n  discusses packaging-line equipment by name to do its\n  logging/scheduling job, so a bare-noun list would self-block the\n  actor's own legitimate proposals whenever a task description\n  mentions conveyors, bottles or labels.\n- Naming the crew-member field `:operator-id` (matching the ISCO title\n  \"...Machine Operators\" literally): rejected -- `docs/operator-\n  guide.md` and this fleet's convention already use \"operator\" to mean\n  the deploying business/entity running the actor, so reusing the same\n  word for the individual crew member would create a confusing\n  collision; `:packer-id` (mirroring the reference's `:converter-id`\n  pattern, and the packaging industry's own term \"packer\" for the crew\n  member who runs case-packing/bottle-filling/labelling equipment)\n  keeps the two senses distinct.\n\n## Test verification\n\nFresh local run immediately before pushing:\n\n```text\nRunning tests in #{\"test\"}\n\nTesting packcoord.actor-test\n\nTesting packcoord.governor-test\n\nRan 30 tests containing 65 assertions.\n0 failures, 0 errors.\n```\n")))
